he last four weeks I  mean a total of about a hundred so it's  a very large number coming in a very  short I don't think we've had 100  countries come to us before so this is  unprecedented and then of course the  speed-up itch is even more unprecedented  globally we've seen governments and  central banks do all they can very  quickly to limit the damage done by the  shutdown of the economy to defeat the  virus despite all that extraordinary  effort how worried are you about the  permanent damage to the economy and a  society that might  come from this crisis I think there is a  substantial risk in that you know this  is a crisis that's help and that's  affecting small and medium enterprises  along with some of the big ones and  getting to them is much harder there is  a lot to be concerned about here I mean  another thing I would like to want to  flag is then while in the past when  there's been a crises and you have all  the stimulus in the system it is a  stimulus which is you want people to go  out and spend you want firms to invest  now but that's not what the other side  of this crisis is which is the health  crises and so but at the same time all  of this stimulus is in the system we  want people to stay at home and not  exactly go out and spend so it's a very  different transmission channel this time  round the hope is that you will keep  people with sufficient income so that  they can meet their needs that firms and  businesses can stay afloat and once we  get past this period of lock down that  that things would recover much faster  but I mean the economic landscape  look very different once we come out of  this lock down and I think there's  tremendous uncertainty you think of  another chief economist of the IMF thank  you very much  thank you Stephanie
 

